Day 2: File Operations (Create, Copy, Move, Delete, Find)
Commands Practiced
mkdir <folder> — create a directory
touch <file> — create empty file(s)
cp <source> <destination> — copy a file
mv <source> <destination> — move or rename a file
rm <file> — delete a file
rm -r <folder> — delete a folder and its contents (required, since rm alone fails on directories)
Wildcards: *.txt matches all files ending in .txt
find <path> -name "<pattern>" — search for files by name
find <path> -type d — search for directories only
2>/dev/null — suppress error output (e.g. “Permission denied” when searching system folders)
head -N — limit output to N results
Key Takeaways
rm does not use a recycle bin — deleted files are gone immediately. Always double-check before running rm -rf.
You cannot delete a directory you’re currently standing inside — must cd .. out of it first.
mv renames when source/destination are in the same folder, and relocates when destination is a different folder.
